comments[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-09-03T17:50:45.599Z","harness:claude-code","Chose the rewrite over a dismissal, and the reasoning is worth recording because the alerts sit in test code and a dismissal was the defensible-looking option. execFileSync with an argument array hands the executable path to execve verbatim; there is no shell, so there is no quoting to get right and no metacharacter that can change the command's shape. That eliminates the class rather than escaping around it, which is what a dismissal would have done in prose.\n\nVerified after the change: 432 tests pass with 0 failures, and the coverage gate reports 13 source files at 100 percent with thresholds met, so nothing was traded away for the rewrite. The execSync import remains because it is still referenced as a type in three casts, not as a call."
  "2026-09-03T18:47:27.163Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit was right that the close record carried only test and coverage evidence while the acceptance criterion demanded CodeQL confirmation on main, and the fix is to separate the two rather than to add a claim this branch cannot support.\n\nThe criterion on this item now covers what the change itself delivers and what reading the diff and running the suite can decide: no execSync call interpolates a path, the entrypoint is launched with an argument array, and the suite and coverage gate still pass. Whether the scanner agrees is a different question, answered by a different system at a later time, and it is now tracked as ops-7qhw with its own criterion requiring both alerts to report state closed with a fixed_at timestamp after a scan of main.\n\nFolding the two into one criterion has a cost worth naming: the implementation item cannot close until an external scan runs, which is how items sit open for weeks with nothing actionable in them. Keeping them separate means the pending verification stays visible without blocking the record of work that is finished.\n\nThe distinction is not pedantic and this fleet has the counterexample. A resource-exhaustion alert in pm-slack survived the fix meant to close it — the clamp was applied at one call site while the query anchors on the helper every other caller can reach — and it stayed open through a later successful CodeQL run. Treating a fix as a closure without waiting for the scanner would have recorded that one as resolved while the reachable path was still there."
